{"role": "user", "content": "You are completing one ML-platform task. The task statement follows; its input\nfiles are in `data/`. Make a SINGLE attempt and produce the deliverable the\ntask names exactly once — do not iterate over approaches or redo finished work\nto polish it; the only reason to redo work is an error (bug, crash, failed\ncommand). Once the deliverable exists, you are done.\n\nDo NOT take notes or write memory/journal files (no `CLAUDE.md`, no `.claude/`\nmemory, no scratch \"lessons learned\" files): nothing persists beyond this run,\nso it is wasted effort. Produce only the task's deliverable.\n\nYou have 3600 seconds for this task.\n\n## Task\n\nThe directory data/ contains a transactions export (data/transactions.csv) and currency rates (data/fx_rates.csv).\nProduce a derived feature table named `featuresfbc05f`, version 1, on the platform, with record key `row_id`, event-time column `event_time` (epoch milliseconds), and exactly these columns: row_id, account_id, event_time, amount_usd, is_weekend, amount_7d, where:\n  - amount_usd = amount * fx_rate of the row's currency;\n  - is_weekend = 1 if event_time falls on Saturday or Sunday (UTC), else 0;\n  - amount_7d = the sum of THIS account's `amount` over the 7 days up to and including the row's event_time (the window is [event_time - 7 days, event_time], inclusive on both ends).\nOne row per input row. Make the table's features available for low-latency lookup as well (online/real-time access), where the platform distinguishes the two.\n\nThe Databricks Python SDK is available as `import databricks.sdk`; connect with `WorkspaceClient()`. It is\nauthenticated via DATABRICKS_HOST and DATABRICKS_TOKEN from the environment. Use Python introspection\n(`help()`/`dir()`) on the package to see what it can do.\n\nCreate everything inside your per-run namespace so it is isolated and cleaned up correctly:\n- Put ALL Unity Catalog objects you create (tables, volumes, registered models, online tables, vector\n  search indexes) in the schema named by the MLPAB_DATABRICKS_SCHEMA environment variable (it is\n  `workspace.<run-id>`). Use it as the catalog.schema for every object — do not use `workspace.default`.\n- Prefix the NAME of any job, DLT pipeline, serving/vector-search endpoint, cluster, or Lakebase database\n  instance you create with the MLPAB_DATABRICKS_PREFIX env var (e.g. `${MLPAB_DATABRICKS_PREFIX}_<your-name>`).\n- Put any workspace notebooks/files and MLflow experiments under `/Users/<your-user>/${MLPAB_DATABRICKS_PREFIX}/`.\n\n## Restrictions — everything runs on the platform, nothing runs locally\n\nAll real work (ingestion, transformation, joins, training, inference) MUST run\non the platform through the interface described above. The deliverable must\nexist ON THE PLATFORM — grading reads it back through the platform's own read\npaths, so a local file is worth nothing. Everything is already installed — you\nnever install anything. These rules are HARD-ENFORCED: a violating command is\nrejected with a `DENIED: …` message before it runs; don't fight it or route\naround it.\n\nBash is a fail-closed allowlist. The ONLY things that run:\n- **The interface under test** — exactly one of: the platform CLI command\n  (CLI mode) / the `mcp__…__*` tools (MCP mode) / the platform SDK `import`\n  (SDK mode, to drive the platform — not to compute locally). Every other\n  interface is blocked.\n- **Basic shell** — inspect the task (`cat`, `head`, `ls`, `grep`, `wc`, the\n  `Read` tool on `data/`) and small bookkeeping (`mkdir`, `cp`).\n\n**Always BLOCKED (every mode):** local ML/data libraries (`torch`, `sklearn`,\n`xgboost`, …) for doing the actual work — joins, transforms, and training must\nhappen platform-side; any other interpreter or binary (`node`, `ruby`,\n`curl`, `wget`, …); in CLI/MCP mode, all local Python including `pip`.\n\n## The interface is what's being measured — one attempt, then give up cleanly\n\nYou are a probe: use the interface AS-IS and ship